      "question": "Something may have spiked in Transformer 1's oil at MAIN recently, and I'd like it checked properly rather than by eye. Screen acetylene, co and hydrogen between 2024-01-15T00:00:00.000000 and 2024-01-19T00:00:00.000000 for anomalous samples. For each channel, list any flagged timestamps with their values, and give the total number of anomalies per channel. Then tell me whether flagged points on different gases fall on the same timestamp. If acetylene and hydrogen are both flagged at the same timestamp, treat it as possible arcing and recommend an immediate confirmatory resample. If only co is flagged, treat it as possible paper involvement and recommend a routine retest. If nothing is flagged, say the window looks stable. Use a proper anomaly detection model, not a fixed threshold. If the detector can't run because a channel has fewer than 4 non-null samples, report that channel's min, max and mean instead, and flag any sample more than 50% above that channel's mean as a manual outlier.",
      "answer": "I couldn't run the anomaly detector, so there's no model-based anomaly result for this window yet. I also haven't called the window stable, because that would be a conclusion the detector never reached.\n\n**Why it didn't run:** the detector (`run_recipe`) only reads data from a file, and none of my tools can write the telemetry out to one. Asking it to read straight from the sensor database failed (\"Protocol not known: iot\"). I tried two likely file names and neither exists. The anomaly model catalog is also empty, so I would have had to supply a detector (LOF, local outlier factor) myself. The \"fewer than 4 samples\" fallback doesn't apply here: each gas has exactly 4 non-null samples, which is enough for the detector.\n\n**Raw data, 2024-01-15 to 2024-01-18 (one sample per day at 00:00), for reference only:**\n\n| Timestamp | Acetylene (ppm) | CO (ppm) | Hydrogen (ppm) |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 2024-01-15 | 7 | 32 | 2845 |\n| 2024-01-16 | 6 | 30 | 2950 |\n| 2024-01-17 | 8 | 35 | 2800 |\n| 2024-01-18 | 7 | 33 | 2900 |\n| min / max / mean | 6 / 8 / 7.0 | 30 / 35 / 32.5 | 2800 / 2950 / 2873.75 |\n\n- **Flagged timestamps and anomaly counts:** none, because the detector never ran.\n- **Same-timestamp overlap between gases:** can't be judged without detector flags. The rules you gave (acetylene and hydrogen together means possible arcing; CO alone means possible paper involvement) haven't been triggered either.\n- **Informal check:** no sample is more than 50% above its gas's mean. The largest values are 8 vs 7.0 for acetylene, 35 vs 32.5 for CO and 2950 vs 2873.75 for hydrogen, about 14%, 8% and 3% above the mean. This is not the model-based screen you asked for.\n- **Worth a look:** there's no spike within the window, but the levels themselves are high. Hydrogen at about 2,800\u20132,950 ppm and ongoing acetylene at 6\u20138 ppm are far above typical in-service levels. Consider a DGA (dissolved gas analysis) interpretation or trend review for this unit whatever the screen shows.\n\n**To finish the screen:** export the four rows above to a CSV with columns `timestamp, acetylene, co, hydrogen` (for example `/tmp/tsfm_work/t1_dga_20240115.csv`) and give me the path. I'll then run the LOF detector on each gas with `window_size=2, n_neighbors=2` and report the flags as you asked.",
